   "text": {
    "L1": "An altitude starts at a vertex and meets the opposite side, or its extension, at 90 degrees.",
    "L2": "It is the perpendicular distance from the vertex to the line of the opposite side.",
    "L3": "In an acute triangle, all three altitudes lie inside the triangle.",
    "L4": "But an altitude does not have to stay inside. In an obtuse triangle, two altitudes fall outside on extended sides.",
    "L5": "Every triangle still has three altitudes, one from each vertex.",
    "L6": "Do not confuse an altitude with a line to the midpoint. The altitude is the one that meets the side at 90 degrees.",
    "L7": "Now choose the angle an altitude makes with the side it meets."
   },
